If an alternative-energy transition can be supported by (1) sufficient firm, low‑carbon electricity capacity (e.g., nuclear and other dispatchable/firm resources) plus operational flexibility, and (2) the grid’s transmission, storage (both power *and* energy capacity), and demand response can reliably meet demand not only during typical short fluctuations but also during rare, extended, large‑area renewable “low-output” periods (i.e., multi‑day deficits), with no unacceptable loss of service for industrial/critical loads—and this is corroborated by real-world operation where fossil fuels do not dominate day-to-day electricity supply for long stretches in comparable national systems—then the conclusion follows that alternative energies can effectively replace fossil fuels without requiring a near-term fossil dispatchability bridge; 

If, instead, the main reliability threat is that widespread, coincident, multi-day renewable shortfalls will occur and—under realistic scaling—grid expansion/interconnection, storage (limited by energy capacity), and demand response cannot cover those deficits without unacceptable service loss (so a firm dispatchable component is still required during those events), then the conclusion follows that alternative energies cannot effectively replace fossil fuels on an all-hours, whole-system basis and that some fossil-based dispatchable component (e.g., natural gas) remains necessary. 

If the evidence does not establish whether multi-day, large-area deficits can be reliably covered under the above constraints, then the question of effective full displacement is unresolved.
